    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_2f4de34c16441aa00069544b95ae3168e502ea2daac750484da28832d4e2259d",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "The separate real- and imaginary-part cumulant analyses and the bivariate generator jointly identify a two-level limit mechanism: logarithmically growing common variances suppress bounded pure and mixed higher cumulants, producing independent normalized Gaussian coordinates, while exact cancellation of odd phase cumulants makes the finite-N imaginary-part density even and removes the real part's leading skew correction, improving its stated convergence order from (log N)^(-3/2) to (log N)^(-2).",
